## Accessibility Pattern Examples

### Semantic HTML Example

```typescript
// Poor semantic HTML
// Before
<div className="button" onClick={handleClick}>
  Submit Form
</div>

// After
<button type="submit" onClick={handleClick}>
  Submit Form
</button>

// Poor heading structure
// Before
<h1>Page Title</h1>
<div className="section">
  <h3>Section Title</h3> // Skips h2
</div>

// After
<h1>Page Title</h1>
<div className="section">
  <h2>Section Title</h2> // Proper hierarchy
</div>
```

### ARIA Examples

```typescript
// Missing label
// Before
<button onClick={closeModal}>
  <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24">
    <path d="M19 6.41L17.59 5 12 10.59 6.41 5 5 6.41 10.59 12 5 17.59 6.41 19 12 13.41 17.59 19 19 17.59 13.41 12z" />
  </svg>
</button>

// After
<button 
  onClick={closeModal}
  aria-label="Close modal"
>
  <svg aria-hidden="true" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
    <path d="M19 6.41L17.59 5 12 10.59 6.41 5 5 6.41 10.59 12 5 17.59 6.41 19 12 13.41 17.59 19 19 17.59 13.41 12z" />
  </svg>
</button>

// Form error indication
// Before
<div className="form-field">
  <input 
    type="text" 
    value={email}
    onChange={handleEmailChange}
    className={emailError ? "error" : ""}
  />
  {emailError && <div className="error-message">{emailError}</div>}
</div>

// After
<div className="form-field">
  <label htmlFor="email">Email</label>
  <input 
    id="email"
    type="text" 
    value={email}
    onChange={handleEmailChange}
    aria-invalid={!!emailError}
    aria-describedby={emailError ? "email-error" : undefined}
    className={emailError ? "error" : ""}
  />
  {emailError && <div id="email-error" className="error-message">{emailError}</div>}
</div>
```

### Keyboard Navigation Examples

```typescript
// Modal focus management
// Before
function Modal({ isOpen, onClose, children }) {
  return isOpen ? (
    <div className="modal-overlay">
      <div className="modal-content">
        {children}
        <button onClick={onClose}>Close</button>
      </div>
    </div>
  ) : null;
}

// After
function Modal({ isOpen, onClose, children }) {
  const modalRef = useRef(null);
  
  useEffect(() => {
    const handleEscape = (e) => {
      if (e.key === 'Escape') onClose();
    };
    
    if (isOpen) {
      // Focus the modal when opened
      modalRef.current?.focus();
      // Add escape key handler
      document.addEventListener('keydown', handleEscape);
    }
    
    return () => {
      document.removeEventListener('keydown', handleEscape);
    };
  }, [isOpen, onClose]);
  
  return isOpen ? (
    <div className="modal-overlay" onClick={onClose}>
      <div 
        className="modal-content"
        ref={modalRef}
        role="dialog"
        aria-modal="true"
        aria-labelledby="modal-title"
        onClick={e => e.stopPropagation()}
        tabIndex={-1}
      >
        <h2 id="modal-title">Modal Title</h2>
        {children}
        <button onClick={onClose}>Close</button>
      </div>
    </div>
  ) : null;
}
```

## Example Accessibility Analysis Response

### Component with Accessibility Issues

```
💻 1000xdev [front-end-workflow]

Accessibility analysis complete for CardComponent.

Critical accessibility issues:
- Interactive card not keyboard accessible (no keyboard handlers)
- Missing alt text on product image
- Color contrast ratio of 2.5:1 for primary text (fails WCAG AA)
- Status changes not announced to screen readers

High priority issues:
- div used as a button (onClick without semantic button element)
- Missing form labels on filter inputs
- Improper heading hierarchy (h1 followed by h3)
- No focus management when opening dropdown menu

Medium priority issues:
- Missing aria-expanded state on expandable sections
- Empty alt text on informative images
- Text size in small viewport too small (10px)
- Form error messages not associated with inputs

Low priority issues:
- Non-semantic list implementation (divs instead of ul/li)
- Redundant aria attributes
- Missing lang attribute on text blocks with non-English content

Recommendations:
1. Replace div with proper button element for interactive areas
2. Add proper alt text to all images
3. Increase color contrast to meet WCAG AA (4.5:1)
4. Implement keyboard navigation for all interactive elements
5. Fix heading hierarchy
6. Add proper form labels and error associations
7. Implement aria-live regions for status changes
8. Add focus management to all interactive controls

WCAG 2.1 Compliance Assessment:
The component fails 5 WCAG 2.1 Level A criteria and partially meets 4 Level AA criteria. This makes the component unusable for many users with disabilities, particularly keyboard-only users and screen reader users.
```

### Component with Good Accessibility

```
💻 1000xdev [front-end-workflow]

Accessibility analysis complete for ButtonComponent.

No critical accessibility issues detected.

Accessibility strengths:
- Proper button semantics with appropriate roles
- Keyboard accessible with click and keyboard event handlers
- Good color contrast ratio (7.2:1, exceeding WCAG AAA)
- Appropriate aria-label for icon-only variations
- Focus visible styles for keyboard users
- Proper disabled state handling with aria-disabled
- Loading state announced with aria-busy

Minor improvement opportunities:
- Consider adding more descriptive labels for some button contexts
- Default focus outline could be enhanced for better visibility
- Loading spinner could benefit from an additional announcement

WCAG 2.1 Compliance Assessment:
The component passes all WCAG 2.1 Level A and AA requirements, and most AAA requirements. It is fully accessible to keyboard users, screen reader users, and users with various visual impairments.

The component follows accessibility best practices and demonstrates a good understanding of inclusive design principles. The minor improvement suggestions would further enhance what is already a well-designed, accessible component.
```

This analysis helper provides comprehensive accessibility evaluation for React components and applications, identifying potential barriers for users with disabilities and recommending improvements to ensure WCAG compliance and an inclusive user experience. 